<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<ResourceDictionary
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
        Step 13 Part B removed InfoBarInformationalStyle, InfoBarSuccessStyle and
        InfoBarWarningStyle. All three had zero call sites since the day they were written.
        MainWindow's ErrorBar is the only InfoBar in the app.

        Step 15 then removed the IMPLICIT Style TargetType="InfoBar" too. A locally-set Style
        replaces the implicit one outright rather than merging with it, and the ErrorBar sets
        InfoBarErrorStyle, so all five of those setters were unreachable. If a second InfoBar ever
        appears WITHOUT an explicit style it will render as stock WinUI - add the implicit style
        back at that point, do not leave it bare.

        Brush.Status.Success and Brush.Status.Warning are now unreferenced. They are kept anyway:
        they are part of the documented semantic palette, not accidental leftovers, and
        they had no real consumer before this either — only the unused styles.

        Foreground is Text.OnStatus, NOT Text.Primary. A status fill is a saturated signal colour in
        both themes, so its label is always near-black. Text.Primary was a genuine contrast bug:
        paper ink on Brush.Status.Error measures 2.78:1 against a required 4.5:1, and it shipped that
        way. Near-black on the same fill is 6.14:1.
    -->
    <Style x:Key="InfoBarErrorStyle" TargetType="InfoBar">
        <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{ThemeResource Brush.Border.Default}" />
        <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="{StaticResource BorderWidth.Standard.Thickness}" />
        <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource Radius.Sharp}" />
        <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{ThemeResource Brush.Text.OnStatus}" />
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="{ThemeResource Brush.Status.Error}" />
    </Style>

</ResourceDictionary>
